+# ============================================================ [MP8] Forklift Certified 8-player support
+#
+# This minigame differs from every previous one. **Each player "owns" a delivery area**.
+# In vanilla spawn_players() the spawn point and the delivery area are both taken with **the same index**, as in
+#     spawn_positions[idx] / delivery_areas[idx] / delivery_area_nodes[idx]
+# The scene only has 4 areas (4 **inline** nodes under DropAreas, not separate .tscn files),
+# so for the 5th player delivery_areas[4] reads out of bounds → null → error reading a property.
+#
+# **Approach (decided by the user 2026-08-17) - no extra areas, teams of two instead.**
+# 8 players = 4 teams x 2 players, and each team shares one delivery area. A crate in the area counts for the whole team.
+# A side benefit of this route is that **the round structure is exactly the same as vanilla** —
+# eliminating 4 teams down to 1 team = 3 rounds, identical to vanilla eliminating 4 players down to 1.
+# (The earlier "top up to 8 areas" approach is scrapped. Don't add areas using probe60's edge-midpoint coordinates.)
+#
+# Issues measured by probe59/60/61 that really do trigger with 8 players.
+#
+#   1. **Crate scatter infinite loop** (the most severe, the server freezes on the spot)
+#      initialize() calls spawn_crates(player count x2), which is 16 with 8 players.
+#      Scatter area 12x12, minimum point spacing 4.0, and in 3000 samples it **never once reached 16**
+#      → vanilla's unbounded `while points.size() < target_size` never exits.
+#      Fixed in crate_manager.gd (configurable scatter area + a bounded while that keeps the best attempt),
+#      with the parameters in `_mp8_apply_crate_profile()` in this file.
+#
+#   2. **Only 4 spawn points**. Teams of two need 8 (two per area, teammates side by side).
+#      The generic top-up route can't be used either — vanilla's 4 points (±16,±16) are ordered [bottom-left, bottom-right, top-left, top-right],
+#      so taking "the midpoint of adjacent points" uses [1][2] for j=1 and [3][0] for j=3, and **both come out as (0,0)**,
+#      right in the middle of the field inside the crate pile. So `mp8_owns_spawns` is set to make the generic top-up step aside, and this minigame manages its own.
+#      (Pitfall #33)
+#
+#   3. **Only 4 blood decals**. Up to 6 players can die in this minigame (3 teams x 2).
+#      spawn_blood_rpc takes one with `get_child(0)` each time, and once they run out it gets null → `decal.get_parent()` crashes.
+#      Fixed per pitfall #14 - **cycle through and reuse the existing ones** instead of creating new nodes.
+#
+#   4. **`_on_crate_numbers_changed` reads `active_players[da.belongs_to_id]` unguarded**.
+#      In the instant after a teammate dies but before the area is set to -2, a crate knocked out of the area triggers this → missing key error.
+#      With vanilla's 1 player per area the window is very narrow, but with teams of two it gets wider. A has() check was added as a safety net.
+#
+# With 4 or fewer players **not a single thing changes**. Each area still has only 1 player, using vanilla's 4 spawn points,
+# vanilla's scatter parameters and vanilla's crate count (player count x2). Teams of two only happen with 5 or more players.
+#
+# Placement is the user's call (hard rule #1). The built-in values for a teammate's secondary spawn are only starting values.
+# Place them by hand in-game with `play.ps1 -ForkEdit`. The save goes to user://mp8_forklift_layout.txt and takes effect automatically next match.
+# Deleting that file = back to the built-in starting values.
+#
+# ⚠️ This pass only does ① support. ② Scoring is untouched — `player_scores = player_count` in check_game_end()
+# has the same disconnect hole as Train Race T28, and with teams of two teammates always tie (0,0,2,2,4,4,8,8),
+# so the gap between 1st and 2nd grows from 2 points to 4. All of this waits until ① passes real-play acceptance.
+# ==============================================================================
+
